                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_mixed:         `<strong>That's the honest read.</strong> Some of the drop is money-fixable — careers services and mobility can be bought — but research depth and the time it takes are structural. Treating it as purely buyable, or purely a marketing problem, is how deans waste an envelope chasing a number.`,
                    coach_r1_money:         `<strong>Careful — “just buy the rank back” underestimates the structural drivers.</strong> Careers and mobility need investment, yes, but research depth and reputation take years, not one intake. Pouring the envelope at the rank alone leaves nothing for the positioning that makes applicants choose you.`,
                    coach_r1_marketing:     `<strong>It is not a marketing problem.</strong> The ranking reflects real career-progression and mobility weaknesses; re-spinning the narrative without fixing the substance fools no one and burns credibility with the board and applicants alike.`,
                    coach_r1_noise:         `<strong>A nine-place fall is not noise.</strong> Internal analysis links each lost place to ~€350k of tuition next intake — an unaddressed drop risks ~€3M of annual erosion within two intakes. Indecision means the board holds the envelope and starts looking for a new dean.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read the drivers honestly AND saw the envelope funds only ~45% of the asks — so you must concentrate a few real bets rather than half-build everything. That clarity is what makes the next three rounds a strategy instead of a wish-list.`,
                    coach_r1_spread:        `<strong>“A proportional share for everyone” is the peanut-butter trap.</strong> Funding 45% of all four asks half-builds each one and bets on nothing. The board chair will ask what MBS can now do that it couldn't before — and you won't have an answer.`,
                    coach_r1_loan:          `<strong>Borrowing your way out of the gap risks the covenant.</strong> More debt against reserves to fund all four asks is exactly what threatens the 2.0% surplus floor. The constraint is real; allocation under it is the whole point.`,
                    coach_r1_lowmoat:       `<strong>You're under-reading the threat to your moat.</strong> Two larger rivals just launched their own sustainable-business and entrepreneurship flagships, with budgets you can't match. Pretending the ground is uncontested is how you walk into an arms race.`,
                    coach_r2_deepen:        `<strong>Deepening the niche is the defensible move.</strong> When better-funded rivals copy your positioning, matching them head-on destroys value — owning a narrower space credibly builds a moat they can't simply out-spend. Now your allocation must prove the claim.`,
                    coach_r2_pivot:         `<strong>A pivot can work</strong> — moving to an adjacent differentiator like AI-for-management or career-outcomes sidesteps the arms race on ground the rivals just claimed. It is riskier than deepening, but it is a genuine strategic choice, not a retreat.`,
                    coach_r2_broaden:       `<strong>Broadening to a generalist top-40 trades your distinctiveness for the middle of the pack.</strong> You'll compete on every dimension against schools with deeper pockets and lose the very thing that made applicants choose MBS. A higher rank as an indistinct school is a fragile win.`,
                    coach_r2_armsrace:      `<strong>This is the arms race you cannot win.</strong> Out-spending two rivals with three times your budget on the exact ground they just entered buys you maybe two ranking places and exhausts the envelope. Is climbing five places worth abandoning your differentiation?`,
                    coach_r2_noexclude:     `<strong>A position without exclusions is just a wish.</strong> If MBS still tries to be top-40 on every dimension while claiming a distinctive niche, you've made no choice. Naming what you will NOT compete on is the strategic act.`,
                    coach_r2_honest:        `<strong>Honest linkage is exactly right.</strong> You named which ranking drivers the positioning fixes and which you deliberately accept losing. That is a board-ready story — it shows the rank response is real, not cosmetic, and it protects the moat.`,
                    coach_r2_both:          `<strong>Promising the niche AND a full climb to 32nd is two strategies, which is none.</strong> You can't deepen a narrow moat and win the generalist rank race at once on €14M. The board will sense the contradiction and so will applicants.`,
                    coach_r2_ignore:        `<strong>Treating ranking and positioning as unrelated misses the point.</strong> Rankings are both an output of quality and an input to demand and pricing. A positioning that ignores which rank drivers it helps leaves your investment unanchored.`,
                    coach_r3_concentrated:  `<strong>That's a real bet.</strong> One pillar clearly leads, so the envelope builds something rather than dusting 45% over everything. The board can now see what MBS chose to win.`,
                    coach_r3_peanut:        `<strong>You've peanut-buttered the envelope.</strong> A little to everyone funds 45% of four plans, so each is half-built and no bet is made. Spreading €14M evenly is the absence of a strategy — where is the bet?`,
                    coach_r3_proves:        `<strong>The allocation proves the positioning.</strong> A concentrated bet that funds the faculty and brand assets behind your niche claim means your money says what your slogan says. That is strategy, not a tagline.`,
                    coach_r3_slogan:        `<strong>Your money says generalist; your slogan says niche.</strong> You declared a distinctive positioning then spent the envelope on generic degree careers with almost nothing on the faculty and brand assets that prove the claim. A trustee will ask which one is real.`,
                    coach_r3_armsrace:      `<strong>You're funding the arms race you can't win.</strong> Pouring degree money into the ground two larger rivals just claimed buys a couple of ranking places and erodes your distinctiveness. The envelope is spent and the moat is gone.`,
                    coach_r3_noexec:        `<strong>Starving exec-ed weakens the margin engine.</strong> The fast-payback online portfolio is what props up the surplus while slower bets mature. Underfunding it makes the covenant harder to hold.`,
                    coach_r3_hollow:        `<strong>You've hollowed out the faculty.</strong> Underfunding hires and letting star professors walk hands rivals a ~€600k chair each, their grant pipeline, and the research reputation underpinning your EQUIS re-accreditation and your innovation positioning. You funded programmes by gutting the asset that makes the school credible.`,
                    coach_r3_retain_underfund: `<strong>You said retain both, but the faculty line is too thin to do it.</strong> Retention is not just a decision, it's a budget — chairs and research support cost money. Fund the faculty pillar properly or the stars walk anyway.`,
                    coach_breach:           `<strong>You've breached the 2.0% surplus covenant.</strong> Front-loading the envelope dropped the operating surplus below the development-loan floor. The lender requests a review, the board panics, and your ranking-recovery story now competes with a solvency story. Phasing the same €14M across three years would have protected both.`,
                    coach_r4_aligned:       `<strong>That's a board-ready answer.</strong> You showed a concentrated bet aligned with the positioning and named the ranking trade-off you accept. The chair sees a strategy, not a spreadsheet — a bet MBS chose to win, not a hope spread across four half-built plans.`,
                    coach_r4_climb:         `<strong>“Back to 32nd whatever it costs” is the arms-race trap restated.</strong> You can't out-spend rivals with three times your budget, and committing to it abandons the differentiation that made applicants choose MBS. The chair will ask what you're betting on, not what number you're chasing.`,
                    coach_r4_everyone:      `<strong>“Something for everyone” is precisely the charge.</strong> Funding all four pillars so no department feels abandoned is the peanut-butter strategy in a suit. The trustee's question stands: what does MBS now do that it couldn't before?`,
                    coach_r4_allbuilt:      `<strong>Half-building everything is not progress.</strong> Funding 45% of four asks leaves four incomplete plans, not one finished bet. The board wants the concentration story, not a defence of dilution.`,
                    coach_r4_moremoney:     `<strong>Asking for a bigger envelope dodges the strategic act.</strong> The €14M cap is the test — leading is choosing under it, not lobbying it away. The board judges how you allocated, not how loudly you asked for more.`,
                    coach_r4_moat:          `<strong>That defends the identity convincingly.</strong> You showed the funded assets deepen the responsible-innovation moat rather than abandoning it — the money proves the claim. That's how you answer the “you've sold out our identity” trustee.`,
                    coach_r4_generalist:    `<strong>Calling identity a luxury concedes the trustee's point.</strong> A top-40 generalist rank without distinctiveness is a fragile win — you'll be one indistinct school among many, out-resourced on every dimension. The moat was the defensible asset.`,
                    coach_r4_sloganpitch:   `<strong>A slogan the money contradicts is a tagline, not a strategy.</strong> Keeping the niche in marketing while the euros go to generic services is exactly the inconsistency the board is probing. Make the allocation match the positioning or drop the positioning.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
